It seems that the method ArrayList.Insert(index, x) inserts x at the specified index of the ArrayList. The Debug.Assert statement ensures that the index of the inserted value does not exceed the old index, i.e., it is "smaller than or equal to" Old_index.
In C# code, you can assert like this:
```cs
System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(condition: New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index,
                                                   "New_arrListIndexOfX is not equal to or smaller than Old_index");
```--------------------Here's the output assert statement:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index, "New_arrListIndexOfX is not smaller than or equal to Old_index");
```

This is the extracted comment from your code:

```cs
// @@@ New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index
```--------------------The given PUT_InsertContract function in the ArrayList.Test Namespace violates this rule. Assertion might be helpful here: "New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index". You are inserting x at position index of arrList and checking whether the index of x in the updated list is smaller than or equal to the old index. If it's not, it implies there was a bug in the implementation. Here's the corresponding Debug.Assert statement:

```cs
// @@@ New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);
```--------------------```cs
// @@@ New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);

// @@@ Old_arrListCount is increased by 1
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount ==  Old_arrListCount + 1);

// @@@ New_arrListCount is the same as Old_arrListCount + 1
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount ==  Old_arrListCount + 1);

// @@@ Old_arrListContainsX is true
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ New_arrListContainsX is true
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ New_arrListLastIndexOfX is greater than or equal to Old_arrListLastIndexOfX	
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ New_arrListIndexOfX is the same as Old_arrListLastIndexOfX
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ Old_x is in the new list
Debug.Assert(New_arrList.Contains(Old_x) == true);

// @@@ New_arrList_[Old_index] == Old_x
Debug.Assert(New_arrList[Old_index] == Old_x);
```

This code represents the conditions that the assertion test cases should satisfy.--------------------The following are the "Debug.Assert" (C#) output statements to match each of the comments:

```cs
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index, "New_arrListIndexOfX is not smaller than or equal to Old_index");
```

The "Debug.Assert" statement checks a Boolean expression and halts execution if the result is 'false'. In this case, it's checking if "New_arrListIndexOfX" is greater than "Old_index". If you check this code, "New_arrListIndexOfX" should always be <= "Old_index" or it will throw an exception, thus fulfilling the commented requirement.--------------------The csharp code corresponding to the comment "New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index" would look like the following:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);
```

The csharp code corresponding to the comment "New_arrListCount is bigger than Old_arrListCount by 1" would look like the following:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);
```

The csharp code corresponding to the comment "New_arrListContainsX is true" would look like the following:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);
```

And this goes on for all commented statements under the method of interest.--------------------The output C# code corresponding to the comments is given below:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_index <= arrList.Count && Old_index >= 0);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);

```
The first assertion checks the precondition that index being inserted is in the array list's acceptable range.
The second assertion validates the invariant of IndexOf (Old_arrListIndexOfX) is less than or equal to the index (Old_index) that the item is getting inserted at. This is a contention point that the contract system should enforce.
The last assertion validates the postcondition of IndexOf also (New_arrListIndexOfX).--------------------```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            // Pre-values.
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Actual method being tested.
            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            // Post-values.
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Debug.Assert statements.
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index); // @@@ New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index

            // Observations.
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}

```--------------------The comment you've asked to extract reads "New_arrListIndexOfX is smaller than or equal to Old_index". According to this, we will use a Debug.Assert to make sure New_arrListIndexOfX is less than or equal to Old_index.
Below is the Assert statement that meets this requirement:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);
